---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Fact from '../components/Fact.astro'
import { AGING_DAYS, HIDDEN_DAYS, programmes, stats } from '../lib/data'
import { amountText, longDate } from '../lib/format'
import { REPO_URL, RS_URL, absolute, generalIssueUrl } from '../lib/site'

const description =
  'Who builds naturefunding.org, how every listing is checked against the funder’s own page, and how to use and correct the open data.'

// A real fact to show what each listing carries.
const example =
  programmes.find((p) => p.id === 'rufford-small-grants' && p.evidence.amount) ??
  programmes.find((p) => p.amount && p.evidence.amount)

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  name: 'How naturefunding.org checks its listings',
  url: absolute('/about/'),
  description,
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Restoration Scope', url: 'https://restorationscope.com' },
}
---

<Base title="How we check" description={description} path="/about/" nav="about" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div slot="band" class="wrap band">
    <h1 class="band__title">How we check</h1>
    <p class="band__lede">
      A grant you can’t trust is worse than no grant: it costs a week of writing for a deadline that has passed. So
      every fact here is read from the funder’s own page and shown with the funder’s words and the date we read them.
    </p>
  </div>

  <article class="wrap prose">
    <details class="toc" data-toc>
      <summary class="toc__title">On this page</summary>
      <nav aria-label="On this page">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#who">Who builds this</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what">What we list</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sources">Where the facts come from</a></li>
        <li><a href="#never-guess">What we never guess</a></li>
        <li><a href="#labels">What the labels mean</a></li>
        <li><a href="#how-often">How often we check</a></li>
        <li><a href="#corrections">Corrections</a></li>
        <li><a href="#open-data">Open data</a></li>
      </ol>
      </nav>
    </details>

    <h2 id="who">Who builds this</h2>
    <p>
      naturefunding.org is built and paid for by <a href={RS_URL}>Restoration Scope</a>, a company that makes
      monitoring and reporting software for restoration projects. We say so openly because a directory that hides
      who runs it is hard to trust.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No paid listings.</strong> Funders cannot pay to be listed, ranked or highlighted.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>No ranking by who pays.</strong> Results are sorted by deadline, soonest first. Whether a funder uses
        Restoration Scope has no effect on its listing.
      </li>
      <li><strong>No account, no email wall.</strong> Search and filters run in your browser.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="what">What we list</h2>
    <p>
      Funding for nature restoration and conservation projects: forests and land, rivers and wetlands, coasts and the
      sea, and the species that live there. We list a programme when its own website says who can apply, where and
      how. A few listings, such as paid tree-planting partnerships, are not grants; their summaries say so.
    </p>
    <p>We leave out, for now:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>funders whose website blocks automated reading, because we could not re-check their facts every day;</li>
      <li>prizes for past work, and programmes whose page only describes an old or suspended round;</li>
      <li>second rounds that are by invitation only, since the first round is where you apply;</li>
      <li>anything we only found in another directory and could not confirm on the funder’s own page.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      So a missing funder may simply not be checked yet. <a href={generalIssueUrl}>Tell us about it</a> and we will
      look.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sources">Where the facts come from</h2>
    <p>
      Each deadline, amount, eligibility rule and region is read from the funder’s own website, or the website of the
      organisation that runs the programme for it: the call page, the guidelines or the programme page. Next to each
      fact we show:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>the page it came from, linked;</li>
      <li>the exact words on that page;</li>
      <li>the date we last checked it, on a tag like <span class="nowrap"><span class="tag">Checked 1 Nov 2026</span>.</span></li>
    </ul>
    {
      example && (
        <figure class="example">
          <figcaption class="example__caption">A real fact, as it appears on the {example.name} page:</figcaption>
          <dl class="example__facts">
            <Fact label="Amount" value={amountText(example.amount)} evidence={example.evidence.amount} />
          </dl>
        </figure>
      )
    }
    <p>
      Other directories and funding guides are useful for finding funders we have missed. We never copy a listing,
      a date or an amount from them.
    </p>

    <h2 id="never-guess">What we never guess</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Deadlines.</strong> If the funder’s page doesn’t give a date, we don’t either. “Not stated” means the
        page doesn’t say, not that there is no limit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>How to apply.</strong> Some funders take open applications, some only fund through partners such as
        country programmes, and some pick their own grantees. We label each one plainly, so you don’t write a proposal
        to a funder that will never read it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="labels">What the labels mean</h2>
    <h3 class="key__title">Is a call open?</h3>
    <dl class="key">
      <div><dt><span class="tape" data-state="open">Open</span></dt><dd>Taking applications now, until the deadline shown.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><span class="tape" data-state="rolling">Rolling</span></dt><dd>Taking applications any time, with no deadline.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><span class="tape" data-state="upcoming">Upcoming</span></dt><dd>Announced, but not open yet.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><span class="tape" data-state="closed">Closed</span></dt><dd>The last call we found has passed its deadline. Check the funder’s page for the next round.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><span class="tape" data-state="none">No call found</span></dt><dd>We found no open or announced call on the funder’s page when we last read it.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="key__note">
      The finder lists open, rolling and upcoming calls first. Closed and no-call listings sit in a folded list under
      them, headed “Not open now”.
    </p>
    <h3 class="key__title">Funders you don’t apply to directly</h3>
    <dl class="key">
      <div><dt><span class="route-label">Through a partner</span></dt><dd>You apply to a partner, such as a country programme, not to the funder.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><span class="route-label">Invitation only</span></dt><dd>The funder picks its grantees. Don’t send a proposal.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <h3 class="key__title">The checked tag</h3>
    <dl class="key">
      <div><dt><span class="tag">Checked 1 Nov 2026</span></dt><dd>The last day we read the funder’s page and found the words we quote.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><span class="tag" data-aging>Checked 20 Sep 2026</span></dt><dd>Over {AGING_DAYS} days since that check. Confirm on the funder’s page before you rely on it.</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <h2 id="how-often">How often we check</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Every day, a job reads the funder pages that are due: most every day, the rest every week or month.</li>
      <li>If a page still shows the words we quoted, those facts get a new checked date.</li>
      <li>
        If a page changed or can’t be read, the date stays where it was. A change becomes a proposed edit, and it
        goes live only after a person approves it.
      </li>
      <li>
        When a deadline date passes, the finder shows the call as closed. It doesn’t compare times of day, so on the
        last day check the funder’s cutoff time.
      </li>
      <li>
        After {AGING_DAYS} days without a check, the tag turns amber:{' '}
        <span class="tag" data-aging>Checked 20 Sep 2026</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        After {HIDDEN_DAYS} days without a check, the listing leaves the finder until we can confirm it again. Its page
        stays up, marked out of date.
      </li>
    </ol>
    {stats.lastChecked && <p class="small">Most recent check: {longDate(stats.lastChecked)}.</p>}

    <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Every programme page has a <strong>Report a problem</strong> link and, for funders, a{' '}
      <strong>Correct this listing</strong> link. Both open a pre-filled issue on GitHub, which needs a free account. We change a listing only after checking the correction
      against the funder’s own page.
    </p>
    <p>For anything else on the site, such as a missing funder or a broken page:</p>
    <p class="actions"><a class="button button--quiet" href={generalIssueUrl}>Report a problem</a></p>

    <h2 id="open-data">Open data</h2>
    <p>
      Our data is licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>: you can reuse it,
      including commercially, if you credit naturefunding.org.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/data.json">data.json</a>: every programme, call and fact with its source, quote and checked date.</li>
      <li><a href="/data.csv">data.csv</a>: one row per call, plus one row for each programme with no call, for spreadsheets.</li>
      <li><a href="/feed.xml">Atom feed</a>: every call with its status; a call that opens, closes or moves its deadline shows up as a new entry.</li>
      <li><a href="/health.json">health.json</a>: the latest run of the checking job: when it ran, and how many pages it read, found changed or could not read.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Quotes from funder pages are their authors’ words and are not covered by our licence; we keep them short and
      always link the source. The code is MIT-licensed and{' '}
      <a href={REPO_URL}>public on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="back"><a class="button" href="/">Find funding</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

<script>
  // The contents list starts folded on phones and open beside the text on wide screens.
  const toc = document.querySelector<HTMLDetailsElement>('[data-toc]')
  const wide = matchMedia('(min-width: 72rem)')
  const sync = () => {
    if (toc && wide.matches) toc.open = true
  }
  sync()
  wide.addEventListener('change', sync)
</script>

<style>
  .band {
    padding-block: 1.75rem 2.25rem;
  }
  .band__title {
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 1.2rem + 2.2vw, 2.75rem);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }
  .band__lede {
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--shell-ink-2);
  }
  .prose {
    max-width: calc(44rem + 2 * var(--gutter));
    margin-inline: 0 auto;
  }
  /* Wide screens: the contents list sits in a sticky rail beside the text. */
  @media (min-width: 72rem) {
    .prose {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 44rem) 16rem;
      justify-content: start;
      column-gap: 4rem;
      max-width: var(--page);
      margin-inline: auto;
    }
    .prose > * {
      grid-column: 1;
    }
    .prose > .toc {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 1 / span 40;
      position: sticky;
      top: 1.5rem;
      align-self: start;
    }
  }
  .back {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
  .key {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0;
    max-width: 66ch;
  }
  .key div {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.3rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
    .key div {
      grid-template-columns: 11rem minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 1rem;
      align-items: baseline;
    }
  }
  .key__title {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .example {
    max-width: 66ch;
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
    padding: 0.25rem 1rem 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .example__caption {
    padding-top: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .example__facts {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .prose .example + p {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }
  /* A route, not a status: an outlined label, unlike the status tape. */
  .route-label {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0 0.4rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    border-radius: 2px;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .key__note {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .nowrap {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .prose p,
  .prose li,
  .prose dd {
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .key dd {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .prose .steps {
    max-width: 66ch;
    margin: 0.6rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.4rem;
  }
  .prose .steps li {
    padding-left: 0.25rem;
  }
  .prose .steps li::marker {
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .prose h2 {
    margin-top: 2.75rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
    font-size: 1.375rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    scroll-margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .prose p,
  .prose ul {
    max-width: 66ch;
  }
  .prose p + p,
  .prose p + ul,
  .prose ul + p {
    margin-top: 0.85rem;
  }
  .prose ul {
    margin-block: 0.6rem 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }
  .prose li + li {
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }
  .prose li::marker {
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .toc {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    padding: 0.6rem 1.25rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface);
    max-width: 66ch;
  }
  .toc__title {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    min-height: 1.75rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .toc__title::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }
  .toc__title::after {
    content: '';
    width: 0.5rem;
    height: 0.5rem;
    border-right: 2px solid currentColor;
    border-bottom: 2px solid currentColor;
    transform: translateY(-25%) rotate(45deg);
  }
  .toc[open] .toc__title::after {
    transform: translateY(25%) rotate(-135deg);
  }
  @media (min-width: 72rem) {
    .toc__title {
      cursor: default;
      pointer-events: none;
    }
    .toc__title::after {
      display: none;
    }
  }
  .prose .toc ol {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(14rem, 1fr));
    gap: 0.3rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }
  .prose .toc li + li {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  @media (min-width: 72rem) {
    .prose .toc ol {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
  .actions {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .small {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
</style>
